  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nEarly This Morning...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms have been near and across the far northern \nforecast area early this morning. These are along a boundary, \nwith forcing from upper energy and 850mb winds helping generate the \nstorms. The upstream trend has been for the overall movement of the \nstorms to shift northeast. Thus, the general threat for heavy rain \nhas diminished for the northeast forecast area.\n\nWill continue with some chance PoPs across the northern forecast \narea.\n\nToday...\n\nAnother piece of upper energy will likely generate another round of \nconvection north of central Indiana early, which will move southeast \nand impact northern portions of the forecast area this morning. Will \ngo with likely PoPs across the extreme northern forecast area with \nchance PoPs across much of the remainder of the northern area. \n\nThere remains some uncertainty on how large this area of storms will \nbe and how far south it will actually get, with latest HRRR trends \nbeing farther north. The 00Z CAM runs keep the heaviest rain north \nof central Indiana as well, so confidence in the Flood Watch \ncontinues to diminish. However, am reluctant to cancel the watch \nsince a complex with heavy rain cannot be ruled out. After \ncollaboration with neighboring offices, will just pull the \nsouthernmost county (Henry) out of the watch. \n\nThis area of storms will likely push the boundary farther southwest \ninto this afternoon. Not much upper support will be around for \nconvection, but can't rule out isolated convection developing. Will \nkeep some slight chance PoPs for parts of the area. \n\nSevere parameters indicate a very low threat for severe weather, but \ngusty winds and heavy rain remain possible.\n\nThe morning rain and the boundary will keep a temperature gradient \nacross the area, with highs in the mid 80s northeast to mid 90s \nsouthwest. Heat indices around 100 will be likely again across the \nsouthwest.\n\nTonight and Friday...\n\nAnother round of upper energy interacting with the meandering \nsurface boundary will bring more scattered convection. Will go with \nchance PoPs many areas. These will carry over into Friday morning \nbefore forcing weakens.\n\nThe boundary will get pushed farther to the southwest, so by mid-\nafternoon only the southwest half of the area will see any PoPs. \nThese will be low PoPs with little or no additional forcing around.\n\nWith the expected movement of the boundary, cooler air will get into \nmore of the area. Highs on Friday will range from around 80 \nnortheast to the lower 90s southwest.\n\nSaturday and beyond...\n\nUpper and surface waves will cause the boundary to meander across \ncentral Indiana into early next week. This will keep chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms around. Confidence on timing and location \nof the highest PoPs remains low with uncertainties on exact \nmovements of the boundary.\n\nSaturday's highs will rebound as the boundary gets pushed northeast, \nwith lower and middle 90s southwest and mid and upper 80s northeast. \nHowever, by Monday, the boundary will be far enough south for highs \nin the mid 70s north to mid 80s south.\n\nAdditional upper waves will keep low chances for rain around mid-\nweek next week. Temperatures will be more seasonable with highs in \nthe 70s to lower 80s.",
